# Single Cobalt Atoms with Precise N-Coordination as Superior Oxygen Reduction Reaction Catalysts

## abstract
A new strategy for achieving stable Co single atoms (SAs) on nitrogen-doped porous carbon with high metal loading over 4 wt % is reported. The strategy is based on a pyrolysis process of predesigned bimetallic Zn/Co metal-organic frameworks, during which Co can be reduced by carbonization of the organic linker and Zn is selectively evaporated away at high temperatures above 800 °C. The spherical aberration correction electron microscopy and extended X-ray absorption fine structure measurements both confirm the atomic dispersion of Co atoms stabilized by as-generated N-doped porous carbon. Surprisingly, the obtained Co-Nx single sites exhibit superior ORR performance with a half-wave potential (0.881 V) that is more positive than commercial Pt/C (0.811 V) and most reported non-precious metal catalysts. Durability tests revealed that the Co single atoms exhibit outstanding chemical stability during electrocatalysis and thermal stability that resists sintering at 900 °C. Our findings open up a new routine for general and practical synthesis of a variety of materials bearing single atoms, which could facilitate new discoveries at the atomic scale in condensed materials.

Nanomaterials exhibit unexpected properties when they are downsized to atomic scale.[1] Investigating the properties of single atoms is fundamentally important but remains challenging in condensed physics and materials science because of the difficulties associated with preparation. To obtain metal SA catalysts, the generation of atomically dispersed metal atoms can be achieved either by physical[2] or chemical methods.[3] The stabilization of metal SAs is mostly based on a bottom-up strategy, during which the metal precursor are adsorbed, reduced, and confined by the defectrich support.[4] Though much effort has been invested, this emerging area is still characterized by significant challenges. First, to fully deposit the well-defined single-metal atoms on the surface of an incorporated support, the concentration of metal atoms should be extremely low to avoid aggregation.[5] Thus far, the requirement of practical and high yield $(>1\ \mathrm{wt}\ \%\$ metal) synthesis of metal SA catalysts has been challenging to satisfy, and the low loading of metal SAs on supported catalysts hinders further study of their macroscopic properties. Second, in most cases the architectural structures of anchor sites for confinement and stabilization of metal SAs on a support remain elusive, and consequently, the coordination environment for metal SAs are inhomogeneous and are poorly defined. In this regard, the precise structure of metal SAs, such as coordination number, dispersion tendencies, and binding mode are difficult to identify and control, delaying the advancement of SA catalysts.
Metal-supported N-doped carbon materials are becoming one of the most promising candidates to replace Pt-based catalysts, in acidic or alkaline medium.[6] As is well-known, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are composed of metalcontaining nodes and organic linkers, which are typically characterized by atomically dispersed metal sites.[7] Pyrolysis of MOFs or ZIFs serves as an important route to metalsupported N-doped carbon materials.[8] However, there are few studies reporting methods for generation of single metal atom-reactive sites because of the drastic reactive processes that occur at high temperature. Metals residing in MOF/ZIF nodes may be partly exchanged with other metals because of the coordination environment provided by the MOF/ZIF linkers, allowing further adjustment of the spatial distance of metal sites. If we can selectively remove the doping metal and reduce the remaining metal in situ, supported metal SAs (or cluster structures) distributed on porous matrices may be fabricated.
Accounting for the same sodalite coordination of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ with 2-methylimidazole,[9] a type of $Z_{\mathrm{{n}/C o}}$ bimetallic MOF (BMOF) with a homogeneous distribution of $Z\mathrm{n}$ and Co can be systematically designed (Supporting Information, Figures S1 and S2).[10] The intentional addition of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ can replace a certain proportion of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ sites and serve as a “fence” to further expand the adjacent distances of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ atoms. The low boiling point $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms (mp $420^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , bp $907^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{,}$ ) can be evaporated away at high temperatures over $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and
Co nodes are reduced in situ by carbonization of the organic linker. Since the molar ratio of $Z_{\mathrm{{n}/C o}}$ added is elevated above 1:1, Co single atoms/nitrogen-doped porous carbon $(\mathrm{Co}\mathrm{SAs}/$ N-C) can be successfully synthesized. The structural adjustability of MOFs also endows the possibility to generalize this strategy to other metal SA materials if the precursors are built up strategically. Moreover, control over the spatial dispersion, metal loading, and coordination structure of SAs can be achieved by this disassembly strategy.
The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image in Figure 1a and high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscope (HAADF-STEM) image in Figure 1b both reveal that the as-prepared $\mathrm{Co}\mathrm{SAs}/N\mathrm{-C}$ structure retains the initial rhombododecahedral shape and size of the starting $\mathrm{Zn}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{1}$ -BMOF $(Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{Co}=1:1\$ ) material. The ring-like selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern taken from an individual rhombododecahedron demonstrated its poor crystallinity (Figure 1 c). To elucidate the form of the Co atoms, we carried out aberration corrected HAADF-STEM measurements (Figure 1d,e) with subangstrom resolution; contrast is characteristic of atomic number.[11] The isolated heavier Co SAs could be discerned in the carbon support because of a different $Z$ -contrast between Co and C. Careful examinations of different areas confirmed the absence of Co cluster and small crystals (Supporting Information, Figure S3). Figure 1 f shows the representative Co, N, and C mappings of $\mathrm{Co}\mathrm{SAs}/N\mathrm{-C}$ by electron energy-loss spectroscopy (EELS). N-mapping indicates that the N heteroatom is homogenously distributed over the entire architecture, resulting from the decomposition of N-containing linkers. To localize the spatial distribution of $\mathrm{Co}$ SAs, a series of HAADF-STEM images with different focal depths were measured. The three selected atoms $(\mathbf{a}_{1},\mathbf{b}_{1}$ , and $\mathbf{c}_{1,}^{\phantom{\dagger}}$ ) in the summed through-focal HAADF images (Figure ${\bf1}\mathbf{g}) $ ) should show different intensities at specific focal depths; these were analyzed to determine the relative vertical positions along the electron beam direction.[12] As shown in the intensity versus defocus function (Figure $\mathrm{1h}$ ), the $\mathbf{c}_{1}$ atom situated between ${\bf a}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{b}_{1}$ along the $x$ -axis is lowest at a focal position along the $z$ -axis. Therefore, it is believed that the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ SAs distribute in a highly dispersed fashion on N-doped carbon in three dimensions, and not only on the surface.
Indeed, the addition of $Z\mathrm{n}$ plays a pivotal role in the formation of $\mathbf{\mathrm{Co}}$ SAs. On one hand, the spatial interval between two $\mathrm{Co}$ atoms can be finely controlled. On the other hand, the leaving $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ sites generate free $\mathbf{N}$ sites during pyrolysis, which are beneficial for the stabilization of Co SAs. As shown in Figure S4 (Supporting Information), the pure Co-containing MOF (ZIF-67) only resulted in $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ nanoparticles (NPs) without the protection of $Z\mathrm{n}$ . This result was also confirmed by the $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) patterns (Supporting Information, Figure S5); a set of well-defined face-centered cubic $\mathrm{Co}$ peaks appear for pyrolyzed ZIF-67. In contrast, utilizing $\mathrm{Zn}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{1}$ -BMOF as a precursor, no peaks characteristic of Co crystals emerged even when the temperature was raised to $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 2 a). The sample at $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ retained atomic dispersion of $\mathrm{Co}$ atoms, as supported by the aberration corrected HAADF-STEM observation in Figure S6 (Supporting Information) and denoted as $\operatorname{Co}\operatorname{SAs}/$ $N$ -C(900). When the temperature was raised to $1000^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the crystallized $\mathrm{Co}$ phase prevailed, as confirmed by the appearance of Co NPs (Supporting Information, Figure S7). Under similar pyrolysis conditions, increasing $Z_{\mathrm{{n}/C o}}$ ratio did not give rise to the aggregation of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ atoms, but a decreased $Z_{\mathrm{{n}/C o}}$ ratio (0.75:1) was not able to offer sufficient confinement and resulted in the formation of $C\mathrm{o-Co}$ bonds (Supporting Information, Figure S8).
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements revealed that the $\mathrm{Co}$ atoms of BMOFs are in a cationic environment with a high binding energy of $\mathrm{Co}2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ at $780.7\mathrm{eV}$ (Supporting Information, Figure S9). For the pyrolysis of ZIF-67, the Co $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ signals down-shifted to $778.8\mathrm{eV}$ and finally exhibited a metallic state corresponding to the reduction process. A similar down-shift of $\mathrm{Co}2\mathrm{p}$ was also observed for $\mathrm{{Zn}_{1}\mathrm{{Co}_{1}}}$ -BMOF, but the valence states of the resulting Co SAs were characterized by a slight positive charge because of $\mathbf{N}$ -coordination.[13] The high-resolution XPS spectrum for N 1s revealed that the dominant nitrogen species after pyrolysis are pyridinic-N and graphitic-N (Supporting Information, Figure S10), which serve as anchor points for Co SAs. The proportion of these two $\mathbf{N}$ species decreased significantly when the pyrolysis temperature increased from $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , indicating that some coordination bonds between $\mathbf{N}$ and Co atoms were broken. At $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ the evaporation of $Z\mathrm{n}$ was tracked by 1) disappearance of the Zn XPS signals (Supporting Information,
Figure S11) and 2) decreasing $Z_{\mathrm{{n}/C o}}$ ratios with respect to pyrolysis time (Supporting Information, Figure S12).
X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) measurements were performed to verify that the isolated dispersed Co SAs are the only form of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ present, and to clarify how they are stabilized. As shown in the $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectra (Figure 2 b), the position of the white line peak for Co SAs/N-C(800) and Co SAs/N-C(900) is located between those of $C_{0}$ foil and $\mathrm{Zn}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{1}$ -BMOF precursor, indicating that the valence state of Co SAs is situated between that of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{0}$ and $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}$ . This observation agrees well with the aforementioned XPS results and reported density functional theory (DFT) predictions, which suggest that single Co sites possess a porphyrinic planar structure.[14] By contrast, the XANES spectrum of Co $\mathrm{NPs}/N$ -C(1000) was closer to that of Co foil, which reveals the dominance of $\mathrm{Co^{0}N P s}$ at $1000^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$
Further structural information about $\mathbf{\mathrm{Co}}$ atoms can be obtained from the extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS). The Fourier transforms $\cdot$ space; Supporting Information, Figure S13) for ZIF-67 and $\mathrm{Zn}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{1}$ -BMOF exhibited similar $\mathrm{Co-N}$ coordination with a peak at $1.60\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , which is ascribed to tetrahedral coordination of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ atoms in a sodalite crystal form. After pyrolysis, the $\mathrm{Co-N}$ coordination peak shifts to a low R-position for the Co SAs/N-C(800) at $1.{\overset{\cdot}{4}}1{\overset{\cdot}{\mathrm{A}}}$ , demonstrating that the main coordination mode of Co varies slightly (Figure 2 c). Importantly, the Fourier transform curves for Co SAs/N-C(800) and Co SAs/N-C(900)
Base on the above results, a mechanism illustrating the formation process of $\mathrm{Co}\mathrm{SAs}/N\mathrm{-C}$ is summarized in Figure 3. During the annealing treatment under a ${\bf N}_{2}$ environment, the organic linkers of MOFs undergo a pyrolysis process to generate N-doped porous carbon.[15] Subsequently, the metal nodes are reduced at high temperature by the generated carbon. Pre-meditated mixing of $Z\mathrm{n}$ can vary the distance of adjacent Co atoms and provide more free N sites, preventing the formation of $C\mathrm{o}{-}C\mathrm{o}$ bonds at high temperature. After the evaporation of low boiling point $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms, the remaining Co atoms anchor on the N-doped carbon support.
Analysis of Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) adsorption– desorption isotherms indicate that $\mathrm{Co}\mathrm{SAs}/N\mathrm{-C}$ maintain a highly open surface area and porous structure (Figure 3 f; Supporting Information, Table S3). These features frame the Co SAs/N-C as an ideal catalyst for the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR), which is paramount for metal–air batteries and fuel cells.[13a,16] The ORR test was studied by steady-state linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) on a rotating disk electrode (RDE) in $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ -saturated $0.1\mathrm{{u}}$ KOH. For better comparison, we prepared N-doped carbon catalyst by the pyrolysis of ZIF-8 (pure $Z\mathrm{n}$ -containing MOF) to evaluate the activity of N dopants in a carbon lattice. However, as shown in Figure S16 (Supporting Information), this as-prepared C-N material exhibited very poor activity in the ORR, indicating that the activity of $\mathrm{Co}\mathrm{SAs}/N{\mathrm{-C}}$ catalysts can largely be ascribed to metal-N-C sites rather than C-N sites. When compared to 1 $\mathrm{\mathop{Co}N P s}/N{-}\mathrm{\mathop{C}}$ and commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalysts, the best ORR activity was achieved by $\mathrm{Co}\mathrm{SAs}/N\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}(900)$ , as judged by the onset $(E_{\mathrm{onset}})$ and half-wave $(E_{1/2})$ potentials in Figure 4 a. The $\mathrm{E_{onset}}$ of Co SAs/N-C(900) was almost the same as that of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ (0.982 V vs. RHE), while its $E_{1/2}$ (0.881 V) was more positive than those of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ (0.811 V) and Co SAs/N-C(800) $\left(0.863\mathrm{V}\right)$ , and also surpassed that of most non-precious metal catalysts reported (Supporting Information, Table S4). This result coincided well with our DFT prediction that $\mathbf{Co-N}_{2}$ species show a stronger interaction with peroxide than $\mathrm{Co-N_{4}}$ (which are dominant in $\mathrm{Co}\mathrm{SAs}/N\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}(800))$ , and promoted the fourelectron reduction process of the ORR (see details in the Supporting Information). The Koutecky–Levich (K-L) plots for Co SAs/N-C(900), obtained from RDE polarization curves at different rotating speeds (Figure 4b), presented nearly parallel fitting lines, suggesting first-order reaction kinetics with respect to $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ concentration and a potentialindependent electron transfer rate. The high electron transfer rate was confirmed by the rotating ring disk electrode (RRDE) tests (Figure 4c), demonstrating a near fourelectron ORR pathway over the single $\mathbf{Co-N}_{2}$ sites.[17] The kinetic current density $(J_{\mathrm{k}})$ at $0.8\mathrm{V},$ derived from the intercept of K-L plots and small Tafel slope of ${75\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}}$ (Figure 4 d), further verified the superior activity of $\mathrm{Co}\mathrm{SAs}/N\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}(900)$ (Figure 4 e). Strikingly, there is no obvious decay in $E_{1/2}$ after 5000 continuous potential cycles (Figure 4 f), demonstrating that the Co SAs/N-C(900) catalyst also exhibits excellent durability. Careful examination of many HAADF images (Supporting Information, Figure S17) confirmed that the atomically isolated $\mathrm{Co}$ atoms were retained after durability tests, which further underlines the stability of the material.
In summary, a general strategy enabling the practical and controlled synthesis of supported metal SA catalysts with atomic dispersion, high metal loading, and excellent molecular accessibility, has been developed and applied to a series of metal $\mathrm{SAs}/N{\mathrm{-C}}$ . The stabilization of Co SAs is strictly related to the N-doped porous carbon support, which is derived from pyrolysis of MOFs and the mixing of Zn as a “fence” to expand the distance of adjacent Co atoms in space. This finding may open up a new family of single-metal or single-molecule materials, which show great potential in condensed science as a result of their properties.
